  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/8/8c/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_98.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Don Blake/Thor is upset about Jane Foster leaving him to work for Dr. Andrews, letting off steam in his medical office.&nbsp; It's all because of <i>Odin</i> that he could not tell Jane that Thor and Don Blake are one and the same -- he <i>forbade</i> him from confessing his feelings of love toward a... <i>mortal</i>!\n\nAt that moment, Odin himself summons his son to Asgard, and there urges his son to \"put all thoughts of the human Jane Foster from out of thy mind!\"&nbsp; After only two panels, Thor turns his back and leaves, saying that \"Even the God of Thunder cannot close his heart upon command!\"&nbsp; Returning home, a heartsick Dr. Blake then decides to head out east to get away from his sorrow.\n\nAt the same time, in a \"lonely, isolated region of India,\" a Professor Shecktor is closing in on finding an antidote for snake venom.&nbsp; Realizing that he'll share in <i>none</i> of the credit for the discovery, his jealous assistant, Klaus (who we later learn is an ex-convict), stages an accident -- allowing a cobra to bite them both while withholding the antidote from the professor.\n\nNews of the \"accident\" soon reaches Bombay, where Don Blake is travelling, and the doctor soon overhears that Professor Shecktor is dying -- that's his old <i>teacher!</i>  He decides to step in as Thor to try and help.&nbsp; He learns from the dying professor that his assistant actually <i>plotted</i> his murder, withholding the antidote from him, all the while not realizing that he (the professor) had been <i>experimenting</i> on the snake -- it was a <i>radioactive cobra</i>!&nbsp; As such, the antidote affected him -- changed him, giving him the abilities and instincts of a cobra!\n\nThor is too late to save the life of the Professor, but he vows to track down the Cobra, \"and <i>destroy</i> him!\"&nbsp; In fact, he finds that the thrill of the hunt has reinvigorated him -- \"the menace of a deadly foe\" and promise of danger and high adventure -- this is <i>exactly</i> what he needs to get his mind off Jane.\n\nThor soon learns that the Cobra has boarded a jet bound for the United States, and with the help of a summoned typhoon, he is able to get to Idlewild Airport before the Cobra, watching him slither up the walls there, finally deciding to follow him and learn his plans before ultimately striking.\n\nCobra heads to a chemical manufacturing plant and demands complete control so he can make gallons of the cobra serum \"to create a vast army of others like myself -- to do my bidding and serve me without question!\"\n\nThor then bursts in, and we get to see a handful of Cobra's interesting abilities to hurl darts and venom (in gas form).&nbsp; Eventually, Cobra tries to blast him with a huge rush of chemical acid-based formula, but it only enrages Thor, and at the bottom of page 9, we see Thor release a \"blood-curdling war cry\" and hurl his hammer, which misses the slippery Cobra, but crashes into the vat of gas, and the vapors blind the Asgardian just long enough to allow Cobra to escape.\n\nAs fate would have it, the Cobra next heads to a nearby doctor's office where he might find \"serums with which I can achieve my ultimate objective!\"&nbsp; Sure enough, it's the office of Dr. Andrews, who is inside with his brand new nurse, Jane Foster!&nbsp; The Cobra demands the serums he seeks, and even though Jane tells her new employer that to do so would be cowardly, Dr. Andrews immediately caves to the Cobra's demands, leaving Jane Foster incensed to find him showing no backbone at all!&nbsp; \"And to think I left Doctor <i>Blake</i> in order to work for <i>you</i>!&nbsp; Although he is lame and unglamorous, you'll never be <i>half</i> the man that <i>he</i> is!\"\n\nSeeing Thor flying outside, she hurls something through the window to attract his attention, and the Cobra prepares to kill her for her insolence.&nbsp; But Thor has taken note and arrives in time to prevent her death.&nbsp; Nevertheless, the Cobra still grabs Jane as a hostage and heads out the window toward a nearby bridge.&nbsp; But Thor quickly finds a way to subdue Cobra and save Jane (giving Cobra time to slip away).&nbsp; Jane <i>immediately</i> leaves Dr. Andrews and heads back to ask Don Blake for her old job back.\n\nThe narrative text implies that it might have been Odin's doing that drew Jane Foster back to Don Blake, \"out of love for his favorite son!\"&nbsp; The pair are together again!\n\nHowever, the Cobra has escaped!",
  "grade_one_liner": "The villain may slither, the nurse may return, but Thor finally stands tall.\nA mundane story finds its spark the moment the Thunder God embraces the fight.",
  "subtitle": "A Serpentine Villain, An Awakened Warrior",
  "review_text": "The story itself is mundane, the villain derivative (in the sense that his powers stem from being bitten by a \"radioactive cobra\"), and the fact that Jane Foster is literally gone from the presence of Dr. Blake for a [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=mere one issue].\n\nThese flaws don't merely weaken the plot; they undercut the emotional stakes the book tries to build around Thor's turmoil.\n\n[ASSET: left | CreateAnArmy | width=20%] Klaus, the ex-convict assistant of Professor Shecktor, who accidentally finds himself affected by the bite of a radioactive cobra, seems so confident in his powers of persuasion that he immediately heads back to the US to order the board of a chemical manufacturing plant to create <i>gallons</i> of cobra serum so that he can create an army, one that will \"do my bidding and serve me without question!\"\n\nNot only does this strike me as a risky and foolhardy plan, it sounds a <i>lot</i> like what the Lizard was planning in last month's <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> ([LINK:1963:ASM_6|ASM #6|2026-12-22]), only with <b>cobras</b>, rather than reptiles (which actually <i>included</i> snakes -- see its page 15).\n\nSo in one issue, plotter Stan Lee has not only copied Spider-Man's origin, he's followed it up with the same evil master plan as last month's villain.&nbsp; It's the sort of repetition that suggests Stan is still experimenting with villain archetypes rather than crafting distinct threats for each title.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=\"Derivative\" doesn't even <i>begin</i> to describe such a dastardly scheme!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | OnlyIfThrown | width=19%] [ASSET: right | FallenHammer | width=23%] Naturally, the issue provides a relatively straightforward battle sequence (pages 7-9), during which time, the Cobra ensnares Thor in his \"unbreakable cobra-cord,\" causing Thor to momentarily drop his hammer in surprise, and it is at that point he mentally notes that it only returns to him if actually thrown.&nbsp; But if only dropped, it remains motionless until retrieved.\n[BR]\n[BR] At the end of page 8, when he goes back to retrieve it (now as Don Blake), he \"stamps it once upon the ground\" to presumably turn back to Thor.&nbsp; While the fact that the fallen weapon is still a hammer is consistent with the content of previous issues, the requirement that he must stamp it on the ground is new.&nbsp; Remember back in [LINK:1962:JIM_83_thor0|JIM #83] (page 7), he only needed to <i>touch </i>the hammer to resume his Thor persona.&nbsp; (Moments like these show how fluid Thor's rules still are, with the creative team adjusting his limitations issue by issue -- something to keep an eye on going forward.)\n\n[ASSET: left | ThorInBattle | width=17%] The battle seemed doomed from the start, with the Cobra forced to throw out one inane defense after another at Thor, just to hold him at bay, which seemed to consign the issue to a mediocre grade...\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=But then, the best panel in the book arrived - the truly <i>spectacular</i> one that closes out page 9.]\n[BR]\n[BR] As he hurls his hammer, Thor shouts out \"By the beard of noble Odin, oh trusty hammer - <i>strike</i>!&nbsp; Strike in the name of <i>Asgard</i>!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] While the panel isn't drawn so wonderfully by artist Don Heck and will never rank in the upper echelon of even mildly famous panels, [SPAN: severity=info; text=it nevertheless captures the Thunder God in a moment of glory, relishing the prospect of battle!]\n[BR]\n[BR] Seeing Thor basking in the thrill of the hunt is truly one of the most invigorating scenes this issue brings to the table!&nbsp; It portrays him (<i>at last</i>) as more than just a powerful figure -- it transforms him into a <b><i>warrior</i></b>, one born to do battle, as if he's finally embracing who he actually is, one no longer bound by a lame physician's restraint.\n\nIs it a shame that it took a [SPAN: severity=warning; text=third-tier villain like the Cobra] to wake up the slumbering God of Thunder?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=Sure.]\n[BR] Nevertheless, even though the panel isn't good enough to pin up on your wall, it still is the first I've seen so far of Thor spouting off such an outburst, so I found it notable, not to mention [SPAN: severity=success; text=the issue's greatest image.]\n\nWhat's also interesting is that Thor strikes \"in the name of Asgard,\" even though Odin's decrees about his interactions with Jane are a large part of his problems.&nbsp; Without realizing it ([SPAN: severity=warning; text=and perhaps this was Odin's plan all along]), it seems to reveal a brand new persona swelling inside him.&nbsp; Even his <i>speech</i> seems more formal, a style reminiscent of the King James translation of the Bible or a William Shakespeare play (both rooted in early‑17<sup>th</sup>-century English rhetoric).\n\nThis is the first time I recall Thor making such an outburst, and indeed, on multiple occasions in this issue, Thor is <i>clearly</i> depicted as <i>reveling</i> in the conflict (e.g. page 5, panel 5, and the first panel on page 9).&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: I must point out that my first exposure to Thor was in <i>Avengers</i> 162, where we see him cry out \"Death to Ultron!&nbsp; Verily, this night we shall <i>avenge</i> our slain comrades, or taste death's bitter cup ourselves!\"&nbsp; Now <i>that's </i>the Thor I know!&nbsp; So I'm thrilled to see the first hint of that here in this issue!]&nbsp; It's as if he's <i>thrilled</i> to be in action, once more pitted against an evil foe (even one so mundane as the Cobra).\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | TouchOfIrony | width=6%] As for the Cobra's move to the doctor's office after Thor first confronts him, forcing him to flee the scene, are we to believe that Dr. Andrews' office just <i>happened</i> to be in close proximity to the \"chemical manufacturing plant\" he (Cobra) first goes to on page 6 (right after deplaning at Idlewild Airport)?\n\nThat is not only <i>ironic</i> (as page 10 indicates), it is right out of the <b>Comic Book Convenience 101</b> course guide.&nbsp; I mean, <i>really</i>, you could not <i>ask</i> for a more ironic ending than <i>that</i> one!&nbsp; It's the kind of coincidence that early Marvel clearly leans on heavily, letting plot threads collide through sheer narrative convenience rather than design.\n\n<i>Of course</i>, Dr. Andrews turns out to be a spineless coward, and <i>of course</i>, Jane decides to head home with her tail between her legs and beg for her old job back, with Stan seemingly suggesting that these events were perhaps laid out by Odin -- that <i>he</i> (rather than fate) led the Cobra to Dr. Andrews' office so that the magazine's <b>RESET</b> button could be pushed.\n\nThe emotional fallout of Jane's departure one issue ago [SPAN: severity=error; text=barely has time to register] before the book restores the familiar status quo.\n[ASSET: center | JaneReturns | width=67%]\n\nBut of course... the Cobra himself <i>escapes</i>!&nbsp; <b><i>Horrors!</i></b>\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nAs I already said above, the main attraction of this issue <i>wasn't</i> the Cobra... and his largely laughable attempt to take down the Thunder God (e.g. <i>Cobra darts?&nbsp; Venom gas pellets?&nbsp; <b>Really</b>?</i>).\n\nAnd it certainly wasn't the convenience factor of having Jane Foster return to Don Blake's practice after only one issue.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=Can you spell \"RESET?\"]\n\nNo, it was the page 9 panel that made the issue - [SPAN: severity=success; text=the sight of the mighty Thor reveling in the joy of battle -- to see him cry out for his hammer to <i>strike</i>... in the name of <i>Asgard</i>!]\n\nThat's an image that will stick with you, even though Don Heck's actual artwork wasn't quite up to the grandeur of the moment.&nbsp; (In fact, if anything, Heck's artwork seems to have <i>regressed</i> a bit.)&nbsp; But nothing could diminish the magnitude of that one spectacular image... the battle cry... the hurled hammer... and <b>what they represent</b>...\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=Thor is an immortal, and this is what he was born to do.]\n\nThat panel -- and Thor thinking in passing on at least two occasions of how great it is to find himself pitted against an evil foe -- both serve to elevate the final grade on this one up a full point beyond serviceable (which is indeed what the rest of this book served up -- a tale that never excites... and even has borderline cringe-worthy aspects).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=It's a textbook example of how a single inspired moment can outweigh an otherwise pedestrian narrative.]\n\nWe can lament the fact that the Cobra gets away in the end, which only means that readers are [SPAN: severity=warning; text=sure to be subjected to him in a future issue] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>buyer beware</i>!)].\n\nBut if one panel can make a book worth reading in a story that otherwise doesn't impress, I'm telling you -- [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=this one did its level best!]\n\nAnd for that, I say <b><i>Bravo!</i></b>\n\nIf nothing else, it proves that even a middling issue can reveal something new about Thor's identity... which seems to be morphing before our very eyes...\n\nThat -- plus a strong <i>Tales of Asgard</i> backup -- nudges this past a mere 5.5.\n[BR] A panel like that makes you immediately want to show it to someone else....\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=And that's the difference...]",
  "side_note": [
    "When Don Blake calls Professor Shecktor his old teacher, this implies a past for Blake.&nbsp; Will tuck this tidbit aside for comparison later.",
    "<b>Tales of Asgard </b>introduces us to Ymir, King of the Ice Giants! (and here Jack Kirby once again is handling the artwork, as opposed to Don Heck in the main story)\n\nWe see Odin in pitched combat against a group of ice giants, but with the aid of his \"magic sword,\" he hurls them into a chasm, plunging them to \"Surtur's fiery domain!\"&nbsp; But one ice giant remains -- Ymir!\n\nOdin's speech is full-on Asgardian, complete with wordy outbursts, and it's actually quite invigorating reading it.\n[BR] \"While breath remains in me, I shall fight you!&nbsp; And by my beard, the victory shall yet be mine!\"&nbsp; ([SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=This is good stuff!])\n\nOdin surrounds him in a ring of fire, trapping him, and returns to his throne!\n[BR] Next issue, we're promised Surtur, the Fire Demon!\n\n<b>REVIEW:</b>\n[BR] Unlike the initial <i>Tales of Asgard</i>, which was largely painting a broad history, this issue actually shows Odin in action, and I sense that Stan Lee is slowly imbuing these Asgardians with a regal quality, particularly in their speech patterns.&nbsp; The narrative very much hints that he is <i>really</i> enjoying this style of writing.&nbsp; I very much suspect that we will soon start seeing this bleed over into the main Thor stories themselves...\n\nBottom Line:\n[BR] A short and mildly entertaining look at an early adventure of Odin.\n[BR] Nice to have some background material to help enrich the mythos!\n\n<b>Perhaps this book is turning a corner.&nbsp; There's certainly a hint of that here!</b>"
  ],
  "ownership": "I was fortunate enough to get a FN+ or so copy that came to me as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $18, and eventually sold on eBay in Feb 2020 for $99, netting me over $68.\n[DEVNOTE: I had another copy which I have kept.]",
  "highlight": "A slithering foe and a returning nurse can't distract Thor from rediscovering the joy of battle."
}